---
import { isPublished } from '../../lib/publish';
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { getCollection } from 'astro:content';

const list = (
  await getCollection(
    'essays',
    ({ data }) => !data.draft && isPublished(data) && data.section === 'koans'
  )
).sort((a, b) => (a.data.order ?? 999) - (b.data.order ?? 999));
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Koans"
  description="A koan is not a riddle and has no answer. It is a designed dead end for the thinking mind."
  path="/koans/"
  noAlternates
>
  <section class="page-head">
    <div class="container">
      <h1>Koans</h1>
      <p>Not riddles. No answer at the back of the book. A dead end built on purpose.</p>
    </div>
    <img class="page-head-banner" src="/assets/page-head-koans.jpg" alt="A lone peak above the mist, one figure sitting and one standing below it" />
  </section>

  <section class="section container" style="padding-top:8px">
    <div class="reading">
      <h2 class="sec-h">What a koan is</h2>
      <p class="body-copy">
        A koan is a record of a conversation between a teacher and a student, kept and later used
        as a tool for practice. The Chinese characters mean “public case” — a case file everybody
        has to face, with no way around it.
      </p>
      <p class="body-copy">
        What a koan does is push reasoning until it runs out, so that you can see the place
        reasoning cannot reach. The teacher asks, “What is the sound of one hand?” He does not want
        an answer. He wants you to hit the wall of that sentence. The conceptual mind is a machine
        that manufactures explanations; feed it anything and it will produce a plausible line. A
        koan is built so that it cannot — and when it finally stalls, another door opens a crack.
      </p>
      <p class="body-copy">
        So this section is not a complete anthology. Each piece does one thing: it starts from a
        case and lands on something you can do today. Not solving it is fine. Carrying it into your
        day is the point.
      </p>
      <p class="body-copy">
        Every piece follows the same route: first the original case, then what it is actually
        forcing you to see, and finally a question to sit with. If the case is opaque, read the
        unpacking. If the unpacking does not land, take the last question and stay with it.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  {list.length > 0 && (
    <section class="section container">
      <div class="paths-intro" style="margin-bottom:40px">
        <h2>The koans here</h2>
      </div>
      <ul class="read-index">
        {list.map((entry) => (
          <li>
            <a href={`/koans/${entry.id}/`}>
              <span class="ttl">{entry.data.title}</span>
              <span class="meta">{entry.data.readingTime} min</span>
            </a>
          </li>
        ))}
      </ul>
    </section>
  )}
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .sec-h { font-size: clamp(28px, 3.8vw, 38px); margin: 14px 0 26px; line-height: 1.28; }
</style>
